        <p>MIO'S COCCHI I.OZENOES.</p>
        <p>Ii ItLESTOWN AND NEWTON.I NO tICE TO CORRESPONDENT Mamas No. 8 (Earlaatown).—Received. 13Ltraes OP Loaf, Newton. We andenitand that Lord Newton h.o been lying • aerionely ill for the put fortnight et hit London reeidenee. I. is ROW coneidered, however. that he It out of danger, and ia progreseing nicely. Tun How T. W. Lion M.P. IS RUSSIA. - The St. Petersburg correspondent of the ° Times" terlegraphing on Stood., MALes that the Emperor lery graciously granted • private audience to the lINGE C01:011 Eon T. W. Leigh, M.P. for the Newton Divieion, and •••14T Door. RILL TRU von. there le ne better Cough 'WWI. 1t.., KBATINO's ewowt Elphlnetone. I.OZOIIOIIS One Wee. t if you MILITARY PRUKOTION. fro. °wee. trY the. hot he/ mho Second Lieutenant Trn. Duncan Legh Whit- and they loaf KW Injure your health the oot . a. „en..., in., tlnglos, eon of Cason WhittingtowregtOr oh °melt, fir. *lmply native led. Sold everywhere h 13. ex. and a great nephew of the late Bishop of St. 1330. tins. Albaree,has been promoted to • lieutenancy in the ...„ let Battalion of the Ki ng's Own Lsocaeter Regiment.</p>
        <p>PILLS FOS Fader! el CONN./LIN PS r. Stet ITIIERN. Oteed-deughtet to the Widow Weleh.oontlnum M prepnre this oelebrated Med rine only original and 'melee Family Reel,. let mlehrltv ler ell Iremnte Complelate hm *Moaned meet enteaelvs and incrwenx demand. Purchasers thendsr• attend to tle fcllowles CAUTION.—ye be that what. thee purchme Is prepared by Mrs. Smllhers, the only perem In roceesalon el it,, MMOMIL lighten. Inion are wrapped In Rho /Peer, anti en the label by Mrs. Smithery tel the 'creme Ed the Sale Me Agents. "W. Muemeoe hlle.. 111. Qnme Vle- rect... formerly el 67. et. P 501.. lemdcre arc engraved Government Stamp. Sold In boxes all.. Rd. each by Inecolste.</p>
        <p>1.0.0. T. Ell". 10111.116 1.01)(11t. This lodge held its ueusl weekly ...ion en Tues- day. The usual kindness was gone through and one memher initiated. The programme for the evening !was a excellent paper an " Alcohol " by Bro. J. ! Woods which provoked some discussion soiong the ! member*. A vote of tkanks wu passed to Bro. Woods for his paper.</p>
        <p>II •1111610.</p>
        <p>What wuconaidared an inter-club run, between! the Crow-lane junior harriers, and the younger por- tion of the hi should Ilarriere,took place on Satur- day Altera.... As a kind of impetus, it was arranged that • medal, eubscribed for by all taking part in the can, should be preaented to If man home. The own° hid exteaded for about 5 Mika, and attar • very pleasant rue, A. Tunstall of the Crow Lane juniors arrived home first, and of comae bellted the medal.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTIMi SKOKIR• CONCRRT AT Newton. ! On Wednesday ••sning,a smoking concert in con- nection with the Newton detschment of the fith VB., &amp;LB. Voluntmrs, was given in the Orderly- room. Newton. Sergeant InArttator Hancock prm I sided over. good •ttend•nce, and AA enjoyable evening wee contributed to by Privates Egan and Devereux, Bugler Raynor, Lancn-Svrgeaot Private Parks, Corporal Kent, and Private Penning- ton.</p>
        <p>BAcwELoai BALL AT NRWTON:</p>
        <p>The third;;;;;l bachelors' ball took place in the Town Mall Newton on Friday craning. The ' ball wen very nicely decanted and bad a pretty appecrance. Th•re were about 50 couples prevent,l and dancing, which commenced about 9 o'clock was kept up with spirit until 4 o'clock next morning. Tho manic wan +applied by Mr. Peanon's string band, nail the catering was efficiently performed by Mrs. King.</p>
        <p>At the Earleatown police station ea Monday morning. before Mr. U.S. F. Borren,7 home Farrell I was chorgod with being drunk and disorderly inl Houghten-streot, on eaturday night, and in de- fault of paying,. fine of 24. Bd. and costs, was sent to gaol for sovon days.—John street, Earlostown, for • similar offense in Alpine- street, en Saturday night, was sent to gaol for 7 day. in default of paying a floe of it.. fid, and costs. --Robert Ilolcroft for being drunk and disorderly In Bndge-street at 12,45 isa Suudity morning, was fined 24. fid and costs. Own Tam maze. The usual weekly sossion of the Mope of Earle.- town lodge of Geed Templars won hold in the Co- operative full, Earlestown on Monday evening. Bro. W. Wilson, C. T, provided, an.l there war • good attendance of members and friends from the Ashton Excelsior and Earleatowe Ebenezer lodges' It was docided to send to the Children.' Orphanage, a doll which was bought and drossed by Om cistern of the lodge, One member wss initiated and several candidatos were proposed. The evening'. programme was then proceeded with consisting of • reading competition, in which Mrs Caner was declared winner. Bro. E. Newman having sub- mitted a roport of the district council held at Bootle, the lalgo Wag cloned in due form.</p>
        <p>LIeCTISIth so tIAYDOCIr. On Toes icy evening in the National schoolroom, asydeck, the Her. J. Cater, formerly of tlaydoct. gave • lecture on tt America sod the Americana," The Rev. F. J. Ireland presided, an I there ism • very isir attendance. The Chairmen, in briefly In- troducingthe lecturer, said that Mr. Cater hail of- fered to do anything to assist the parish, and it was suggeatod then eoinistlaing of that kind could be taken up. Th• lecturer then proceeded with his discourse. which wa• rendered more interesting by the lime light views which wore exhibited of he places of interest to be visited after arriving on the American Continent. lie entered into the home end politicsl life of the Yankee, and gene some amusing accounts of his peculiarities, an compared with English customs. At the close a ni,te of thanks was scourged the lecturer on the motion of th• 1t.,. F. J. Ireland, seconded by Mr. E. Jack-</p>
        <p>Sr. JOHN'S CHURCH. E•III.NirrOWN.</p>
        <p>I My the invitation of the KNV. T. It and Nils. Matthews the officers and choir of St. John's church land the teachers of the Sunday school, were enter- tamed to tea in the Mission Hall on Monday even- ing. There were about SO present, and a met enjoyable tea was partaken of. After tea, the lin, T. It. Matthews addressed thorn present on the I work in which each of them we. engaged, and alluded briefly to the difficulties which had arisen ' in connection with the resignation of the organist, I Mrs. Latimer. It. afterwards, on babelf of the teachers and choir, presented an electroplated I teapot to Mr. and Mrs. Clough, who were teschere in the school, and to Mr. and Mrs. Mayhew, the former of whom was. teacher and member of the chair, on the occasion of their marriage. Mr. Matte hews addressed room kind word, of encouragement to the recipient., each of whom suitably responded. The califen were afterwards cleared, and the re- , mainder of the craning was sociably spent. ; LONDON .sro _NORTH W IeNTNIHN WADON WoliNS</p>
        <p>OFFICIO STAFF 1,1,01101,</p>
        <p>The sir dal dinner in connection with the London and North Western Company's Wagon Works office staff took place on Saturday evening at the Gros- venor Hotel,Mancheater. The party, which included several of the foremen, left Earlestown in a special saloon by the 1.28 p.m train, and on arrival at Cretonopolis dispersed to view the placsol interest I. the city, several undertaking a short trip by water from Victoria bridge. Dinner W. served at six o'clock, and being of. oubstantial character and excellently arced was much enjoyed. After the cloth had been removed, Mr. T. Ormond presided, and Mr. C. W. Emmett occupied the vice choir. Several toasts W.&gt; proposed, and duly honoured, including "the London and North Western Railway Company,'' The Toreson, The office," " The visitors. A numlotr of songs were rendered by Messrs. S. Swann, J. Finch, J. Owen, C. 11. Lea,and J. H. Swoon, and a thoroughly enjoyable evening was spent. Sir. J. F. Roberts officiated St rho piano The return journey woo commenced at II o'clock, sod Earlesown was reached shortly after 12.</p>
        <p>THOII IN hEALTH.</p>
