        <p>THE h;XAMINEII,. FRIDAY</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EA RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>!Br AN OCCASICSAL.C.MMIP .NDIRNT).</p>
        <p>WWI In very far removed Mom Kai Mailmen, yet newe of the distress which is being experienced there by reason of the famine reaches ue, even in this out of the way place. w• hear of relief fund. being promoted, and of handsome donations being I made with. view of rlleviating mine 01 the suffers I ing, hut! cannot find that anv more has boon made in this neighbourhoo I yet. Or courm, I except the munificent donation of Menem. McCorquodale Co. It will be remembered that a short time ago, a house te house collection was made for the relief ot Armenians, and I think the ladlee who took an active pert in that, could not work foes more worthy object than the relief of .starving people who owe allegiance to the eameCrown as ourselves-</p>
        <p>I entice that the Woolen's Liberal Association at 'their lest meeting which was held to the Uo.opera- ties 11611, last week, had under consideration the vestion of " Children end public houses "—A cleverly written paper on the subject was read by toned the rasnibms,and seine appalling figuren were given as te the extent to which child drunkenness existed in thin highly civilised country of ours. It was aid that, in eight years, 5 000 children wider 10 years of age were found drunk, and arrested, In one City alone. If this be true, things aro in a shocking state, and the wooer a bill to prevent any- thing of the kind, is brought in, the better it will he for public morals.</p>
        <p>! Thin brings an now to the temperance question. I Fern long time past, I have often wondered what has become of the teetotal organination. which at one time aide themselves felt in Earlestewn. I remember the time quite well, when a week', mission on temperance matter., was prosecuted, and enthusiastic meeting. were held in the various schools, &amp;c., in the town. The result of those Mission. meet have been of an enduring character, a. for several years nothing of the hind has been attempted. There appears to be steeling of apathy in Tellaiel, to united action, and thus each temper- ance orgenseation depends upon its own immediate circle.</p>
        <p>Judging from the attendance at the trademmen's ball,l Would may that the proettion an 4 premium- Lion of an ambulance litter to th• Maria Council I. ammured, and I hope that the time is not far die- tint when the Council will mete their way to build the mortuary, So often wked for, in which Lb. !atm.), be kept.</p>
        <p>Th• police bane introduced a ee•eible arrange- ment, in regard to themeelvee. No all know that a pelicemae has to be heavily shed, it he is to be proof against damp, and coneoguent cold feet. Thus, in the dead of eight,when we are aeleep, and the burglar I. on th• prowl,the sound of the police- man's tramp in the only thing which disturb, the silence. To prevent this and for other purposes. th• police have secured he their shoes Some Oldie rubber bare, which altogether removes th• tool tramp, and maken the tub of the night marauder a little more difficult and dangerous.</p>
